          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been tired and light-headed for months, my heart races on stairs, and my periods are heavy. Is that urgent, what could iron deficiency do to the blood, and which ICD-10 chapter covers anemia?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nThe tool rated this as routine, meaning it did not match any emergency pattern in its table. That is not a clearance. If you feel faint or pass out, have chest pain, or your heart races at rest or does not settle, seek medical care promptly. Symptoms that have lasted for months still deserve a clinician's review, and it is reasonable to book one soon.\n\n**What iron deficiency can do to the blood.** The iron-deficiency anemia summary (General clinical reference) says that low iron often causes fatigue, breathlessness on exertion, palpitations, dizziness, pale skin, and brittle nails. Onset is usually gradual, and the body compensates well, so people often attribute months of tiredness to other causes. The same summary says the blood count shows the pattern, ferritin is the main test of iron stores, and iron studies can clarify results that look normal. It does not say what caused any particular person's iron loss, and it does not address heavy periods specifically. Those are good points to raise with a clinician.\n\n**ICD-10 chapter for anemia.** The ICD-10-CM Chapter 3 summary (US clinical modification, NCHS and CMS) covers blood and blood-forming organs, codes D50 to D89. Iron-deficiency anemia is at D50, under nutritional anemias. Coding is used for records and billing, not for deciding what is wrong with you.\n\n**What the sources did not cover.** None of the passages assessed your specific symptoms, explained why your periods are heavy, or said what tests you need. A clinician can look at your history, order a blood count and iron studies, and decide on next steps.",
